A||||7||Predator||blown head gaskets||||5||
Z||000000||Predator||01-03-2001||11:34 AM||||This is a update to a previous post I made on my installation of a Turbo kit on my 1985 Fiero GT. The car only has 20,000 miles. After the install as I was driving down the road smoke started coming out the back. A lot of smoke. I had the car towed home, and today I got the results from a shop on what is wrong. They are saying that the Turbo blew the top of the engine off, or in other words caused two blown head gaskets. I never ran above 9 pounds of boost for a very short period of time. I talked to Kevin and went over the install to see if I had done anything wrong, and we could find nothing that would have caused this to happen. First off I am scared to fix it because until I find out why, it could happen again. Second has anybody done this, and how hard is it to replace the head gaskets? My car has been up for two months now and I have to get it running. Any help will be greatly appreciated. I can't afford thousands and thousands of dollars to get it fixed.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.usefulcomputerinfo.com/Fiero4.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Predator||01-03-2001||04:03 PM||||Anybody got any ideas what could cause the head gaskets to go? The engine only has 20,000 original miles on it. The auto place states the Turbo, but I am having a hard time beleaving it since I never really got in it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.usefulcomputerinfo.com/Fiero4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||TheFiero64||01-03-2001||04:41 PM||||It can go if the engine overheats.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BBBAD GT||01-03-2001||04:56 PM||||What kind of fuel mods have you done (injectors/fuel pump/chip)?  What kind of timing retard do you have, if any?  Is it intercooled (if so, how much air is getting to the intercooler)?<P>Head gaskets could go because of number of reasons, but since you are using forced induction, its either not enough fuel or too much timing (or both).  Did you hear it ping at all before it actually let go?  I seriously doubt the boost alone would pop head gaskets, since its only at 10lb of boost.  Could you of started with only 5lb of boost via wastegates?  Maybe a good dynotune with a wideban O2 sensor is in order (or at least an egt meter).  <P>Nobody said forced induction is easy....<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Predator||01-03-2001||05:39 PM||||It is the kit from Design1Systems. It comes with a chip to retard the timing under boost. The wastegate and pretty much everything is preset. It even has a knock sensor that I checked and it is working. The injectors are the stock ones. They are supposed to handle up to 225 hp. I never hit it that hard. I never heard and still don't hear any pinging. I got the car back from the shop that was supposed to do a compression test, and they told me when I picked it up that they took the radiator cap off at the thermostat and when they cranked it, it blew antifreeze. In fact it blew it on my car, and they didn't even clean it up which really ticked me off since the paint job is mint. They are basing the two blown head gaskets they reported on this. They never did the compression or leakdown test so I am back at square one. In a nutshell they didn't want to fool with it, and this way they can say they looked at it. Anyway I'm back at square one.  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.usefulcomputerinfo.com/Fiero4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Raydar||01-03-2001||06:01 PM||||If I start my Formula with the thermostat cap off and the thermostat out, it blows antifreeze out, too. Then the belts sling it everywhere. I kinda don't think that this is a valid test. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||BBBAD GT||01-03-2001||06:29 PM||||I agree with steve, not a very scientific method.  I would drain the oil, and see if it looks like a milk shake.  You could rent compression gauges/radiator pressure gauges at stores like autozone.  <P>Do you have a boost/fuel pressure gauge?  Those are two essential gauges you need for a forced induction motor.  Maybe your fuel pump isn't adequate (either not enough voltage or just old), and you are losing fuel pressure on boost, a gauge would catch that. <P>You have no idea what your A/F ratio is, so I'd take it to get dynoed with a wide ban O2 meter, run conservative timing (you really need to know your total timing at boost, can somebody read that chip of yours?), and get an adjustable fuel pressure gauge to you can tune up/down your A/F ratio (not the best method, but if you need fuel, you need fuel).  <BR>Good luck.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gonzo_442||01-04-2001||08:31 PM||||if you start a fiero with the thermo and cap off, and it spews coolant, the only thing it proves is your cooling system has coolant in it and it does get to the engine. in addition, depending on how long it was run like that, you may need to add more coolant  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>i'm with Bad on this one, get some guages, run the comp tests (or find someplace that will actually do them) and check out the oil. Sounds like you might want to find a good shop if you don't want to do the puzzle solving yourself.<BR>--<BR>g||||reg||1
